PytTorus build-time patch for Torus (https://github.com/PaulMcMillan-Astro/Torus),
made against commit 0e8ac96, the commit the Torus/ submodule points to.

Upstream reports fatal errors by printing a message and calling exit(), which
kills the Python interpreter. setup.py applies this patch to a copy of
Torus/src/utils in the build directory and compiles with -D_exception_handling_,
so these errors throw C++ exceptions instead, and PytTorus turns those into
Python exceptions:

  Err.h      Stop #undef-ing _exception_handling_. When it is defined,
             TorusError() throws TorusExcept for fatal errors (i < 0).
             Warnings (i >= 0) still print and set toruserrno, which Fit.cc
             relies on. Upstream's old exception branch threw for warnings
             too and did not declare toruserrno.
  WDMath.cc  MathError() throws std::domain_error.
  CHB.cc     Cheby(x, y, np, N) throws std::invalid_argument for N > 50
             (it called exit(0)).

Without _exception_handling_ the patched files behave exactly like upstream.
To apply to an upstream checkout: git apply torus-exception-handling.patch

diff --git a/src/utils/CHB.cc b/src/utils/CHB.cc
index ac20e79..cef310e 100755
--- a/src/utils/CHB.cc
+++ b/src/utils/CHB.cc
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include "Pi.h"
 #include "CHB.h"
 #include <cstdlib>
+#include <stdexcept>
 
 using std::cerr;
 
@@ -94,7 +95,13 @@ Cheby::Cheby(const Cheby& Ch) {
 ////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
 Cheby::Cheby(double * x, double * y, const int np, const int N) {
   NChb = N;
-  if(NChb>50) { cerr << "too many coeffs in Cheby\n"; std::exit(0); }
+  if(NChb>50) {
+#ifdef _exception_handling_
+    throw std::invalid_argument("too many coeffs in Cheby");
+#else
+    cerr << "too many coeffs in Cheby\n"; std::exit(0);
+#endif
+  }
   s = 0;
   getchebc();
   e1 = new double[NChb];
